<h2>4. Indian Summer Heat: A Real Problem for Editing Laptops</h2>

<p>This isn't discussed enough. Indian summers hit 40-45°C in most cities. Your laptop's thermal design assumes 25°C ambient temperature. When the room is 38°C, your GPU and CPU thermal-throttle much faster, which means:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Renders take 30-50% longer</strong> in a non-AC room during May-June</li>
<li><strong>Timeline playback drops frames</strong> because the GPU throttles down</li>
<li><strong>Your laptop's lifespan shortens</strong> significantly with sustained high-heat operation</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Solutions that actually work:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Edit in an AC room.</strong> This is the single best "upgrade" you can buy for ₹30K (a 1-ton inverter AC). Seriously.</li>
<li><strong>Laptop cooling pad</strong> with fans (₹800-1,500 on Amazon). Not magical, but drops temperatures by 5-8°C.</li>
<li><strong>Elevate the rear of the laptop</strong> even with a book — improves airflow noticeably</li>
<li><strong>Schedule heavy renders at night</strong> when it's cooler (and electricity is cheaper in some states)</li>
</ol>

