<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Can I edit 4K video on a laptop with 8 GB RAM?</summary>
<p>Technically yes, with proxy workflows. But you'll be frustrated constantly. Resolve and Premiere both eat 5-6 GB just by opening. Add a 4K timeline and you're swapping to disk. Spend the ₹2,500 to add a second RAM stick — it's the cheapest upgrade that makes the biggest difference.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is MacBook Air good enough for video editing?</summary>
<p>The M3 Air with 16 GB handles 4K ProRes beautifully in both FCP and Resolve. It throttles under sustained heavy loads (30+ minute exports, heavy Fusion work) because it has no fan. For 90% of YouTube/wedding editing work, it's surprisingly capable. Just don't try to grade 8K RAW on it.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>RTX 3050 vs RTX 4050 — is the upgrade worth ₹15,000 more?</summary>
<p>Yes. The RTX 4050 has 6 GB VRAM vs 4 GB on the 3050, plus DLSS 3 and better hardware encoding (NVENC). In Resolve, the jump from 4 GB to 6 GB VRAM means you can grade 4K timelines without constant GPU memory warnings. It's the single best ₹15K you'll spend.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Do I need an external monitor for color grading on a laptop?</summary>
<p>If you're doing professional color grading for clients — yes, absolutely. Laptop displays (even good ones) are usually 60-70% DCI-P3 coverage. A calibrated external monitor like the BenQ PD2705U (~₹35,000) or even a Dell U2723QE (~₹30,000) makes a real difference. For YouTube content? Your laptop display is fine.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Should I buy a gaming laptop or a business laptop for editing?</summary>
<p>Gaming laptop, every time — at least until you cross ₹1.2L where MacBook Pro enters the picture. Business laptops at ₹60-80K have Intel Iris Xe or very weak GPUs. They'll run Premiere (slowly) but DaVinci Resolve will be genuinely unusable. The dedicated GPU in a gaming laptop is non-negotiable for serious editing work.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is AMD Ryzen better than Intel for video editing?</summary>
<p>For DaVinci Resolve: doesn't matter much — both are fine because Resolve is GPU-bound. For Premiere Pro: Intel's Quick Sync Video hardware encoding is slightly better optimized for Adobe's export pipeline. In practice, at the same price point, buy whichever laptop has the better GPU and more RAM. The CPU brand difference is negligible.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Can I use my editing laptop for gaming too?</summary>
<p>Yes — that's the beauty of gaming laptops as editing machines. An RTX 4050/4060 laptop handles modern games at medium-high settings. Just remember that gaming sessions in hot Indian rooms without AC will thermally stress components you also need for paid editing work. If your laptop is your livelihood, treat it gently.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>How long will a ₹60,000 editing laptop last before I need to upgrade?</summary>
<p>With proper care (thermal management, regular SSD health checks), a mid-range editing laptop from 2024-2026 should serve you well for 3-4 years of 1080p-4K editing work. The GPU will be the first bottleneck as software adds more AI features that need more VRAM. Budget ₹3,000-5,000 per year for a RAM or SSD upgrade if needed.</p>
</details>

